.emb-root {
    --space-1: 4px;
    --space-2: 8px;
    --space-3: 12px;
    --space-4: 16px;
    --space-6: 24px;
    --space-8: 32px;
    --space-12: 48px;
    --space-16: 64px;

    /* How far anything anchored to a screen EDGE must sit from that edge.
       Not part of the 4px spacing run above -- it is not a design rhythm, it is a
       hardware clearance, and it is deliberately blunt.

       THE UNIT CHANGED UNDER THIS NUMBER, and that is the whole story of its history.
       Authored as 150 when the panels scaled by pixel count, so 150 authored px was about
       150 DEVICE px and cleared the cutout. At referenceDpi 160 one authored px is one dp,
       so 150 became 150dp = 394 device px -- 73% of a 1080-tall landscape screen (#243).

       52 is the measured value, not an estimate. Read off a Pixel 5a over USB, 2026-08-23:

           ROTATION_90 : nonDecorInsets=[136,0][0,63]   <- 136 device px on the LEFT
           ROTATION_270: nonDecorInsets=[0,0][136,63]   <- 136 device px on the RIGHT

       136 device px / 2.625 px per dp = 51.8dp, so 52 clears it and 50 does not (50dp is
       131 device px, five short). Note the side FLIPS with rotation direction, which is why
       this is spent on every edge rather than the one the API happens to name: Unity's
       Screen.safeArea reported the cutout on the LEFT while Android's mAppBounds and Sam's
       own eyes put it on the RIGHT (2026-08-21). Sit far enough in that the side stops
       mattering. A static margin right on every device beats a computed inset right on some.

       These two classes were commented out on effect-model-merge and restored 2026-08-23.
       While they were dead, `character-create` -- which carries .emb-bleed -- ran edge to
       edge, putting "Choose your hero" under the status bar and Cancel under the gesture
       bar. The classes are not decoration; they are the only thing insetting that modal.

       RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if a device with a cutout deeper than 136 device px is supported,
       or referenceDpi moves off 160, recompute this. It is the only place it is written
       down, and tooling/scripts/audit_edge_safety.py fails if either class loses its body. */
    --edge-safe: 52px;

    /* THE SMALLEST A CONTROL A FINGER HAS TO HIT MAY BE. Android's floor, and since
       99b179b1 an authored px IS one Android dp, so 48 here is literally Google's 48dp —
       before that change no USS number was comparable to that guidance at all and this
       token could not have existed.

       A token rather than 48 written thirteen times, because thirteen copies is how the
       old ones drifted: the sweep for #244 found .ms-gear at 44, .db-chip at 32,
       .hero-tile-badge at 34 and .new-character-button at 40, every one of them authored
       under the old scale where the number meant nothing checkable.

       USE THIS, NOT A BARE PIXEL, on anything pressed. tooling/scripts/audit_touch_targets.py
       flags a bare px under 48 on any selector it can prove is interactive, and reads this
       token's own value so the token cannot quietly drop below the floor it names.

       UI Toolkit sizes BORDER-BOX, so this is the touch target with no adjustment for the
       rule's own border-width.

       RETIREMENT TRIGGER: #247 wants element sizes to step per screen class. When that
       lands this stops being one number and becomes the smallest step in a run — update
       the audit with it, since the audit currently assumes a single value. */
    --touch-min: 48px;

    /* THE COMPACT STEP OF THE TYPE SCALE. These are the PHONE's values, and they are the base
       because the phone is the floor — every other screen class steps UP from here, at the
       bottom of this file. See "SCREEN CLASSES" there before changing any of them: a value
       edited here moves the phone only, and its medium/expanded partners have to move with it
       or the run stops being a run. */
    --type-wordmark: 44px;
    --type-display: 36px;
    --type-h1: 28px;
    --type-h2: 22px;
    --type-body: 18px;
    --type-label: 16px;
    --type-caption: 14px;
    --surface-0: rgb(11, 14, 19);
    --surface-1: rgb(20, 25, 34);
    --surface-2: rgb(30, 37, 48);
    --surface-3: rgb(42, 51, 64);
    --ink-1: rgb(237, 241, 246);
    --ink-2: rgb(154, 167, 182);
    --accent: rgb(240, 166, 60);
    --positive: rgb(75, 208, 123);
    --negative: rgb(229, 72, 77);
    --warning: rgb(245, 166, 35);
    --info: rgb(76, 154, 255);
    --mana: rgb(61, 165, 245);
    --rarity-common: rgb(154, 167, 182);
    --rarity-rare: rgb(61, 123, 245);
    --rarity-epic: rgb(162, 75, 229);
    --rarity-legendary: rgb(240, 166, 60);
    --class-neutral: rgb(138, 143, 152);
    --class-emberward: rgb(229, 96, 42);
    --class-tidecaller: rgb(31, 138, 143);
    --class-gravekeep: rgb(126, 107, 158);
    --class-verdant: rgb(75, 154, 84);
    --class-tempest: rgb(16, 14, 150);
    --class-clockwright: rgb(114, 101, 61);
    --class-stonewarden: rgb(107, 129, 141);
    --class-nightveil: rgb(35, 5, 78);
}

/* ── Hardware edge clearance ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   --edge-safe above is the distance anything anchored to a screen EDGE keeps
   from it. It reaches a screen through these two classes and nowhere else — no
   screen restates the value, so retuning it is one line in one file.

   MARGIN, not padding, for chrome. A parent's padding does not reach a
   position:absolute child, and every piece of screen chrome is absolutely
   positioned. Measured on a Pixel 5a, 2026-08-21: a parent padding of 136 moved
   the identity plate, the footer and the shard counter by exactly 0; the same
   136 as margin moved all three by 136. Margin also COMPOSES with the offset the
   screen authored, so `left: var(--space-6)` keeps meaning 24 and this adds to
   it — the design gutter sits inside the hardware clearance rather than being
   replaced by it.

   Applied per element rather than to every child of .emb-root, because a flow
   child in a column would take the clearance on its TOP and BOTTOM too, and
   three of those on a 1080-unit panel eats the screen. */
.emb-edge {
    margin: var(--edge-safe);
}

/* A layer that must PAINT to the panel edge — a scrim, a modal backdrop — spans
   the panel and insets its own CONTENT instead. This is why one class serves both
   the bare scrims and the modal containers: paint to the edge, keep content in. */
.emb-bleed {
    padding: var(--edge-safe);
}

.unity-base-dropdown__label {
    color: var(--unity-colors-dropdown-text);
}

/* ── SCREEN CLASSES — how big an element is, by how big the screen is (#247) ───────────────
   Sam, 2026-08-23: "we need breakpoints on some of the elements, specifically the SIZE of the
   elements. So far, we only have breakpoints on layout, not on the size of buttons."

   He was being generous. There were no breakpoints at all — what existed was per-component
   solves (CardWall promoting tiles by available width, LayoutHeroGrid solving a column count)
   and one content-driven modifier. Nothing keyed on the screen, so every element shipped one
   size for every device.

   THE KEY IS THE SHORT AXIS, IN dp. Android calls it smallest width; here the game is
   landscape-locked (NOR-283) so it is always the panel's HEIGHT, and it is always the scarce
   one. The Pixel 5a gives 411dp of it against 914dp of width — height is what runs out, so
   height is what decides. Measured, not assumed: see the recipe in
   ~/.claude/projects/.../memory/editor-game-view-does-not-reproduce-device-dp.md, because the
   Editor's own Game View reports 1800dp here and would put every desk test in `expanded`.

   THE BREAKPOINTS ARE MATERIAL 3's, deliberately not invented:
       compact    sw < 600     the phone. Pixel 5a = 411
       medium     600..839     large phones, small tablets
       expanded   sw >= 840    tablets, and the Editor/desktop at 1800

   ONE MULTIPLIER IS IMPOSSIBLE, so the scale steps token by token. #247 asked whether sizes
   should step per-breakpoint or scale as a group from one number. That is not a preference
   here — it is a constraint, measured in a live panel 2026-08-25:

       calc(var(--probe-base) * 2)   resolved width = 4000.00   <- declaration DROPPED
       var(--probe-base)             resolved width = 10.00

   4000 is the panel width, i.e. the element fell back to `auto`. USS parses calc() and then
   discards it, silently, which is the worst of both. So `font-size: calc(var(--type-body) *
   var(--ui-scale))` cannot work and the runs below are written out in full. DO NOT "simplify"
   this into a multiplier without re-running that probe.

   THE BASE IS COMPACT, above, and these two blocks only ever step UP. Mobile-first: a phone
   that never matches a class still gets a complete, correct scale, so a bug in the applier
   degrades to "the phone size everywhere" rather than to nothing.

   WHAT PARTICIPATES: the type scale and the touch floor, which is what #247's acceptance asks
   for. THE SPACING RUN DELIBERATELY DOES NOT — --space-* is what buys the room the bigger type
   spends, several layout solves are written against it (LayoutHeroGrid's HeroGutter and
   HeroChrome are literal copies of --space-2 and its border), and moving both at once would
   make any regression impossible to attribute. Left for a follow-up with its own evidence.

   APPLIED BY: ScreenClass.cs, which installs itself and needs no scene edit. Exactly one of
   these three classes is on .emb-root at any time.

   THESE BLOCKS MUST STAY BELOW `.emb-root`, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. The
   base tokens are declared on `.emb-root` — a single class — and so are these. Equal
   specificity, same element, so SOURCE ORDER is the only thing deciding the winner. Move
   either block above the other and the screen classes silently stop applying: no error, no
   warning, every screen just renders at phone size forever.

   RETIREMENT TRIGGER: audit_touch_targets.py reads --touch-min and assumes a SINGLE value.
   It now has three. If that audit is not taught the run, it is checking the phone only —
   which is the safe direction, but not the whole answer. */
.emb-sw-medium {
    --type-wordmark: 50px;
    --type-display: 40px;
    --type-h1: 32px;
    --type-h2: 24px;
    --type-body: 20px;
    --type-label: 18px;
    --type-caption: 15px;
    --touch-min: 52px;
}

.emb-sw-expanded {
    --type-wordmark: 56px;
    --type-display: 44px;
    --type-h1: 36px;
    --type-h2: 26px;
    --type-body: 22px;
    --type-label: 20px;
    --type-caption: 16px;
    --touch-min: 56px;
}

/* ================================================================================================
 * NO VISIBLE SCROLLBARS, ANYWHERE (Sam, 2026-08-01 ruling — [[no-visible-scrollbars-ruling]]).
 * Wheel, touch-drag and keyboard scrolling all still work; this hides only the track+thumb
 * chrome, which reads as generic UI Toolkit default rather than as this game's visual language.
 *
 * ONE RULE REPLACED SIX (#161, 2026-08-25). The same block had been written out per ScrollView in
 * character-roster.uss (x2), collection.uss, mainmenu.uss and shop.uss — and FIVE OF THE SIX WERE
 * INCOMPLETE. Only #roster-scroll listed all five classes; .shop-scroll listed one. A horizontal
 * scroller was visible in the collection, the shop and the deck-image strip, because each copy
 * hid whichever scroller its author happened to have on screen that day.
 *
 * THE CLASS IS DOUBLED ON PURPOSE. `.unity-scroller.unity-scroller` matches exactly what
 * `.unity-scroller` matches, but scores (0,2,0) instead of (0,1,0), so it beats the default
 * runtime theme without needing an ancestor. An ancestor-scoped form (`.emb-root ...`) would miss
 * ConnectionFailedScreen.uxml, whose root is .mm-modal and which is built into the panel at
 * runtime rather than under a screen root.
 *
 * THIS RULE IS A BACKSTOP, NOT THE MECHANISM — 2026-08-30. It only bites where content never
 * overflows. `ScrollView` writes its scroller's `display` as an INLINE style driven by
 * `scrollerVisibility` (default `Auto`), and inline beats every USS selector: the doubling below,
 * an id selector, anything — USS has no `!important`. So the moment a surface actually overflows,
 * the scroller comes back and this rule is powerless. Consolidating six copies into one (#161) was
 * right; it did not make any of them work.
 *
 * THE MECHANISM IS THE UXML ATTRIBUTE: `vertical-scroller-visibility="Hidden"` /
 * `horizontal-scroller-visibility="Hidden"` on the `<ui:ScrollView>`, which sets what the internal
 * logic reads. Every new ScrollView gets both from the start. A `ListView` does not expose the
 * property — set it on its inner ScrollView in C#, as CollectionController does.
 *
 * NEVER read this rule's presence as evidence a surface is covered. That read is what let the bug
 * survive two fixes; the shop shipped a visible scroller on a build four days after #161 landed.
 *
 * RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if the no-visible-scrollbars ruling is ever relaxed for one surface, this
 * rule is the thing that has to gain an exception — do NOT re-add a per-id copy, which is how it
 * drifted into six divergent versions the first time.
 * ================================================================================================ */
.unity-scroller.unity-scroller,
.unity-scroller--vertical.unity-scroller--vertical,
.unity-scroller--horizontal.unity-scroller--horizontal,
.unity-scroll-view__vertical-scroller.unity-scroll-view__vertical-scroller,
.unity-scroll-view__horizontal-scroller.unity-scroll-view__horizontal-scroller {
    display: none;
}
